      "writtenOn": "2023-01-06T15:26:17Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I agree that it isn\u0027t neat to expose the NULL-ending element to the Rust code but OTOH, mandating a `Vec` in the API isn\u0027t much better IMO as it forces callers to use the type in their code, even if they don\u0027t require the flexibility of it.\n\nInstead, could we take a `\u0026[\u0026CStr]` (the caller can always create a `Vec` and pass it as a slice), document the need for the NULL and validate\n```\nif !partition_names.last().map(|p| p.is_null()).unwrap_or(false) {\n    return Err(AvbImageVerifyError::InvalidArgument)\n}\n```\n? IMO, the very best way to approach this would be to use a dedicated array-like type (e.g. [`null_terminated`](https://docs.rs/null-terminated)) but this seems overkill here.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 352,
        "startChar": 25,
